[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged on subject lines ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject))
and marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Read by tenant and channel, the reports hold two
problems with two different outcomes. Each one has its own ticket.

<Callout>
#4471 closed on 5 October. The parser is fixed. The booking-v3 hint is won't fix: the form is retired.
</Callout>

## What #4471 held

<ProblemTable
  ticket={4471}
  repo="radxhunter/mega-s01e03"
  issues={{ parser: 1, hint: 2 }}
  outcome={{ parser: 'fixed: fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales', hint: "won't fix: booking-v3 form retired" }}
  oneDayIssue={3}
/>

Counted from `reports-4471.json` at render time, the way the desk counts: one id, one report (the
export repeats some), each report where it is filed, by tenant and channel.

## The problems

- **`parser`**: the shared parser read every date month first, and every tenant writes the day
  first. 7 April became 4 July. Every channel, every tenant.
- **`hint`**: booking-v3's form printed `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People typed what the hint
  said, and the form read it the tenant's way, day first. Only the `form` channel of booking-v3
  tenants. The parser fix did not touch it; retiring the form ends it.

## Why intake missed it

`meta.intakeTag` is a guess from the reporter's browser. The column "intake tagged otherwise" counts
the reports in each problem that intake filed under the other name.

## Lessons

- A subject line says how a report feels, not what broke. Read the tenant and the channel before you merge.
- One ticket marked known can hold problems with different fixes, and one with no fix at all.

